# Rust Dependency Management Best Practices

Proper dependency management is crucial for building maintainable, secure, and efficient Rust applications. These guidelines will help you manage dependencies effectively throughout your project's lifecycle.

## Cargo.toml structure

- **Organize your manifest** in a consistent format

  ```toml
  [package]
  name = "my-project"
  version = "0.1.0"
  edition = "2024"
  authors = ["Your Name <your.email@example.com>"]
  description = "A concise description of your project"
  license = "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
  repository = "https://github.com/username/my-project"

  [dependencies]
  # Runtime dependencies listed alphabetically
  serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
  tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }

  [dev-dependencies]
  # Test-only dependencies
  mockall = "0.11"
  proptest = "1.0"

  [build-dependencies]
  # Build script dependencies
  cc = "1.0"

  [features]
  # Feature flags
  default = ["std"]
  std = []
  ```

- **Use proper semantic versioning** constraints

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Exact version - brittle but reproducible
  exact-crate = "=1.2.3"

  # Compatible with API - most common (^1.2.3)
  compatible-crate = "1.2.3"

  # Minimum version but compatible API
  minimum-crate = ">=1.2.3, <2.0.0"

  # Latest patch version (1.2.*)
  patch-crate = "~1.2.3"

  # Any version - avoid in production!
  any-crate = "*"
  ```

- **Split dependencies appropriately** by their usage

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Runtime dependencies - used in your actual code
  serde = "1.0"

  [dev-dependencies]
  # Testing dependencies - not included in your binary
  criterion = "0.4"
  mockall = "0.11"

  [build-dependencies]
  # Used only during the build process
  bindgen = "0.63"
  ```

## Version pinning strategies

- **Pin dependencies for reproducible builds**

  ```toml
  # Cargo.toml - Use explicit versions
  [dependencies]
  tokio = "1.25.0" # Explicit version
  serde = "1.0.152" # Explicit version
  ```

  ```toml
  # Use Cargo.lock for applications
  # Example command to update only specific dependencies
  # $ cargo update -p tokio --precise 1.25.0
  ```

- **Use version ranges for libraries**

  ```toml
  # For libraries, use compatible versioning
  [dependencies]
  tokio = "1" # Compatible with any 1.x version
  serde = "1.0" # Compatible with any 1.0.x version
  ```

- **Consider cargo-lock2hash for deterministic builds**

  ```
  # Generate a hash of your Cargo.lock file to ensure consistency
  $ cargo lock2hash > Cargo.lock.hash

  # Verify the hash hasn't changed
  $ cargo lock2hash --verify Cargo.lock.hash
  ```

## Feature flags

- **Design feature flags** for optional functionality

  ```toml
  [features]
  default = ["std", "json"]
  std = ["serde/std"]
  json = ["dep:serde_json"]
  postgres = ["dep:sqlx", "sqlx?/postgres"]
  sqlite = ["dep:sqlx", "sqlx?/sqlite"]
  ```

- **Use conditional compilation** with features

  ```rust
  // Only compile this code when the "postgres" feature is enabled
  #[cfg(feature = "postgres")]
  pub mod postgres {
      use sqlx::postgres::PgPool;

      pub async fn connect(url: &str) -> Result<PgPool, sqlx::Error> {
          PgPool::connect(url).await
      }
  }

  // Include different code based on feature combinations
  #[cfg(all(feature = "std", feature = "json"))]
  pub fn parse_json_file(path: &str) -> Result<Value, Error> {
      let data = std::fs::read_to_string(path)?;
      serde_json::from_str(&data)
  }
  ```

- **Document your features** clearly

  ```rust
  //! # Feature flags
  //!
  //! - `std`: Enables standard library integration (default)
  //! - `json`: Enables JSON serialization/deserialization (default)
  //! - `postgres`: Enables PostgreSQL database support
  //! - `sqlite`: Enables SQLite database support
  //!
  //! Note: Database features are mutually compatible.
  ```

- **Use weak dependencies** for optional features

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Only included when the "wasm" feature is enabled
  wasm-bindgen = { version = "0.2", optional = true }

  [features]
  wasm = ["dep:wasm-bindgen"]
  ```

## Security considerations

- **Use cargo-audit to check for vulnerabilities**

  ```bash
  # Install cargo-audit
  $ cargo install cargo-audit

  # Check your dependencies for known vulnerabilities
  $ cargo audit

  # Ignore specific advisories if necessary
  $ cargo audit --ignore RUSTSEC-2021-0000
  ```

- **Pin dependency versions** for security-critical code

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Pin exact versions in security-critical applications
  crypto-lib = "=1.2.3"
  ```

- **Use cargo-deny for policy enforcement**

  ```toml
  # deny.toml
  [licenses]
  allow = [
      "MIT",
      "Apache-2.0",
  ]
  deny = [
      "GPL-3.0",
  ]

  [bans]
  # Crates that should not be used
  deny = [
      { name = "unsafe-crypto" },
  ]
  ```

- **Consider reviewing dependencies** before adding them

  ```bash
  # View full dependency tree
  $ cargo tree

  # Check code size contribution
  $ cargo bloat --release
  ```

## Workspace management

- **Organize multi-crate projects** as workspaces with all crates in a `./crates` directory

  ```toml
  # Root Cargo.toml
  [workspace]
  default-members = ["crates/cli"]  # Default binary crate
  members = [
      "crates/core",
      "crates/cli",
      "crates/web",
      "crates/common",
      "crates/macros",
  ]

  # Set shared dependencies for all workspace members
  [workspace.dependencies]
  serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
  tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
  tracing = "0.1"

  # Specify a resolver version for the workspace
  resolver = "2"
  ```

- **Share dependencies across workspace members**

  ```toml
  # In member crate Cargo.toml (e.g., crates/cli/Cargo.toml)
  [dependencies]
  # Use the shared dependency from [workspace.dependencies]
  serde.workspace = true
  tokio.workspace = true

  # Local dependencies from the workspace
  my-project-core = { path = "../core" }
  ```

- **Use virtual manifests** for clean organization

  ```
  my-project/
  ├── Cargo.toml     # Virtual manifest (workspace definition only)
  ├── crates/
  │   ├── core/
  │   │   └── Cargo.toml # Core crate manifest
  │   ├── cli/
  │   │   └── Cargo.toml # CLI crate manifest
  │   └── web/
  │       └── Cargo.toml # Web crate manifest
  ```

- **Use cargo workspaces commands**

  ```bash
  # Build all workspace members
  $ cargo build --workspace

  # Run tests for all workspace members
  $ cargo test --workspace

  # Clean all workspace members
  $ cargo clean --workspace
  ```

## Dependency optimization

- **Trim unnecessary dependencies** to reduce bloat

  ```bash
  # Analyze your dependency tree
  $ cargo tree

  # Check for unused dependencies
  $ cargo install cargo-udeps
  $ cargo udeps

  # Find duplicated dependencies
  $ cargo tree --duplicate
  ```

- **Minimize feature creep** by being selective

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Only enable the specific features you need
  tokio = { version = "1", features = ["rt", "macros", "time"], default-features = false }
  serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"], default-features = false }
  ```

- **Avoid deep dependency chains** when possible

  ```bash
  # Analyze dependency depths
  $ cargo tree --depth=3

  # Identify heavy dependencies
  $ cargo tree -e features
  ```

- **Consider size optimization** for specific targets

  ```toml
  # In .cargo/config.toml
  [target.'cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")'.dependencies]
  wee_alloc = "0.4"

  [profile.release]
  lto = true
  codegen-units = 1
  opt-level = "z"  # Optimize for size
  strip = true     # Strip symbols
  ```

## Vendoring dependencies

- **Vendor dependencies for offline builds**

  ```bash
  # Vendor all dependencies into vendor/ directory
  $ cargo vendor

  # Create configuration for using vendored dependencies
  $ cargo vendor > .cargo/config.toml
  ```

- **Configure vendored dependencies**

  ```toml
  # .cargo/config.toml
  [source.crates-io]
  replace-with = "vendored-sources"

  [source.vendored-sources]
  directory = "vendor"
  ```

- **Consider git submodules** for specific dependencies

  ```toml
  [dependencies]
  # Point directly to a repository with a specific revision
  my-lib = { git = "https://github.com/username/my-lib", rev = "abc123" }
  ```

## Semantic versioning and publishing

- **Follow semantic versioning** when publishing

  ```
  MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH

  - MAJOR: Breaking API changes
  - MINOR: New features, no breaking changes
  - PATCH: Bug fixes, no breaking changes
  ```

- **Document version changes** in a changelog

  ```markdown
  # Changelog

  ## 0.2.0 (2023-03-15)

  ### Added

  - New feature X
  - Support for Y

  ### Changed

  - Updated dependency Z to 1.0

  ### Fixed

  - Bug in function A

  ## 0.1.0 (2023-01-10)

  - Initial release
  ```

- **Test version compatibility** before publishing

  ```bash
  # Check your crate before publishing
  $ cargo publish --dry-run

  # Verify with cargo-semver-checks
  $ cargo install cargo-semver-checks
  $ cargo semver-checks
  ```

## Common mistakes

- **Overly restrictive version pinning**: Using exact versions for all dependencies in libraries
- **Dependency sprawl**: Including too many dependencies for minimal functionality
- **Feature explosion**: Enabling all features for dependencies when only a few are needed
- **Ignoring updates**: Not periodically updating dependencies for security fixes
- **Circular dependencies**: Creating cyclical dependency relationships
- **Unnecessary re-exports**: Re-exporting every dependency's types
- **Inadequate testing across versions**: Not testing with minimum supported versions
- **Forgetting to specify edition**: Not declaring the Rust edition in Cargo.toml
- **Mixing wildcard versions**: Using "\*" versions in production code

